.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH rqx-perf 1  "perf " 
.SH NAME
rqx\-perf \- Where the work grows faster than the data, ranked by reach
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrqx perf\fR [\fB\-\-level\fR] [\fB\-\-by\fR] [\fB\-\-sort\fR] [\fB\-\-top\fR] [\fB\-\-min\-depth\fR] [\fB\-\-profile\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-threads\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-tui\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-mouse\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-\-motion\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR] [\fB\-\-progress\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-\-hyperlinks\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fIPATH\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Where the work grows faster than the data, ranked by reach
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-level\fR \fI<LEVEL>\fR
relaxed, standard, strict or pristine
.TP
\fB\-\-by\fR \fI<BY>\fR [default: function]
One row per function, per file, or per directory
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
function
.IP \(bu 2
file
.IP \(bu 2
dir
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-sort\fR \fI<SORT>\fR [default: weight]

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
weight
.IP \(bu 2
order
.IP \(bu 2
io
.IP \(bu 2
reach
.IP \(bu 2
sampled
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-top\fR \fI<TOP>\fR [default: 20]
How many rows
.TP
\fB\-\-min\-depth\fR \fI<MIN_DEPTH>\fR [default: 0]
Skip anything whose loops nest less deeply than this
.TP
\fB\-\-profile\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
A CPU profile, so a finding nothing ran stops counting. A V8 .cpuprofile, a Go pprof and collapsed stacks are read by content
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit one JSON object per row
.TP
\fB\-\-threads\fR \fI<N>\fR
Worker threads. Defaults to the machine\*(Aqs parallelism
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Read settings from this file, layered over any `rqx.yaml` found
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-tui\fR
Never open the interactive interface; bare `rqx` prints the report instead
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-mouse\fR
Leave the mouse to the terminal. The session never captures it; the flag is still accepted so an existing invocation keeps working
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fI<COLOR>\fR [default: auto]
When to colour output; `auto` follows NO_COLOR and whether stdout is a terminal
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto
.IP \(bu 2
always
.IP \(bu 2
never
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-motion\fR \fI<MOTION>\fR [default: on]
Animation in the interactive interface
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
on
.IP \(bu 2
off
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR
Do not draw the progress bar on stderr; `\-\-quiet` implies it
.TP
\fB\-\-progress\fR \fI<PROGRESS>\fR [default: auto]
How a scan reports progress on stderr; `plain` prints a line every two seconds for a job log
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto: A bar on a terminal, nothing in CI or when stderr is redirected
.IP \(bu 2
bar: A bar whenever stderr is a terminal, CI included
.IP \(bu 2
plain: A line on stderr every two seconds and at each phase, for a job log
.IP \(bu 2
none: Nothing
.RE
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Say more: \-v adds the configuration, the cache and every skipped file to the report; \-vv also prints stage timings and git commands on stderr
.TP
\fB\-\-hyperlinks\fR \fI<HYPERLINKS>\fR [default: auto]
Make file:line in the report a link that opens in your editor; `auto` only where the terminal is known to support it
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto: On a terminal that is known to open them
.IP \(bu 2
always: Whenever the report is coloured
.IP \(bu 2
never: Never
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
[\fIPATH\fR] [default: .]

